course: dictionary
lesson: 7035
title: Partisan: taking sides
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Partisan is a word about taking sides. A partisan person strongly supports one party, team or idea, and often does not stop to be fair to the other side. «Partisan means strongly supporting one side; bias means leaning towards one side instead of being fair.» So a partisan is someone who has picked a side and keeps cheering for it.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. Words like «strongly supporting» or «only one side» tell you a partisan person is not being fair. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «The newspaper gave only one side of the story, so it was partisan.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Partisan can also be a noun, a strong supporter of a party, person or idea: «She is a partisan of the local football club.» In history, a partisan was a fighter in a secret army who fought soldiers who had taken over their country, often from forests and mountains.
fact: Partisan is an adjective (a describing word) and a noun (a naming word).
fact: As an adjective, «partisan» means strongly supporting one party or side, often without being fair to the other side.
fact: «Non-partisan» means not taking sides. «Bipartisan» means supported by both sides.
fact: In history, a partisan was a secret fighter who opposed soldiers who had taken over their country, especially in the Second World War.
exercise: «Partisan means strongly supporting one side; bias means leaning towards one side instead of being fair.» What does partisan mean? | Running very fast | Leaning towards nobody | *Strongly supporting one side | Being fair to everyone
exercise: A news show gives only one side of an argument and never the other. Which word describes it? | *Partisan | Non-partisan | Bipartisan | Famished
exercise: Which word means not taking sides? | Glum | Partisan | Famished | *Non-partisan
test: In history lessons, what was a partisan? | *A fighter in a secret army against soldiers who had taken over their country | A football fan who sings the loudest | A map of the forest | A teacher who marks the homework
test: Dad says, «I am a partisan of the green team.» What is a partisan here? | A kind of lazy person | *A strong supporter of a team | A fighter in a secret army | A map of the hills
exam: Which is the best check that you have understood a word from its context? | Skip the whole text | Look only at the first letter | Guess the longest word | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
